<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Banjar Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1526 CE – 1860 CE · capital Banjarmasin (later Martapura)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Muslim sultanate of southern Borneo, a major pepper exporter, abolished by the Dutch in 1860 after the Banjarmasin War began.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1526 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-suriansyah">Raden Samudra</a>, a prince of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/negara-daha">Negara Daha</a>, won a civil war with help from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/demak-sultanate">Demak</a>, accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and took the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Suriansyah. He founded his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banjarmasin">Banjarmasin</a> near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barito-river">Barito River</a>. The royal chronicle, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hikayat-banjar">Hikayat Banjar</a>, tells the story.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Banjar grew rich on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>, exported to Chinese, English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> traders in the 17th century, as well as gold and diamonds. After a Dutch attack in 1612 the court moved upriver to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martapura">Martapura</a>. Islamic scholarship flourished, notably under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-arsyad-al-banjari">Muhammad Arsyad al-Banjari</a> in the 18th century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Adam issued a law code in 1835.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> gained increasing control through treaties. A succession dispute after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Adam's death led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banjarmasin-war">Banjarmasin War</a> from 1859, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pangeran-antasari">Pangeran Antasari</a>, and the Dutch abolished the sultanate in 1860, though resistance continued until 1905.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/banjar-sultanate.jpg" alt="Banjar Sultanate" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Aday · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banjar_Sultanate_Flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1526 CE – 1860 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Banjarmasin (later Martapura)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Suriansyah, Adam, Pangeran Antasari</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Banjar Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sultanate of Banjar, Banjarmasin Sultanate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1693&amp;focus=banjar-sultanate" aria-label="Banjar Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7861 2302 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Banjar Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8181 2558L8168 2746M8181 2558L8063 2691M8181 2558L7959 2663"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8168 2746h0M8063 2691h0M7959 2663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8181 2558h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8181 2558h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Banjarmasin. <a href="/atlas?year=1693&amp;focus=banjar-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
